<p>Albertville sits on Sand Mountain, where the clay-and-loam soil drains better than the heavy red clay down in the valley — but "better" doesn't mean "good." When gutters fail, water still pools where it shouldn't: against foundations, around landscaping, soaking into fascia boards.</p>

<p>We pulled gutters off a 1970s ranch on McCord Avenue last fall that had been up for 48 years. The original sectional aluminum had seams leaking at every joint, hangers rusted through, and 6 inches of compacted debris that had basically become soil. The fascia underneath? Soft as cardboard for 30 feet on the back of the house. That's what decades of "it's fine for now" gets you.</p>

<p>A lot of Albertville homes were built in the 60s, 70s, and 80s. Good solid houses, but the gutters are tired. Others have gutters that were installed cheap — undersized 5-inch when they needed 6-inch, downspouts spaced 50 feet apart when 30 would barely be enough.</p>

<p>Blue River Gutters serves Albertville the way folks here expect: show up when we say, give you a straight price, do quality work. We've been at this for over 20 years and we're not going anywhere.</p>
{% endset %}

